@startuml

title ores.workflow.service Component

set namespaceSeparator ::

namespace ores #F2F2F2 {
    namespace workflow #F2F2F2 {
        namespace service #F2F2F2 {
            namespace app #F2F2F2 {
                class application {
                }
                class host {
                }
            }
            namespace config #F2F2F2 {
                class options <<struct>> {
                    +logging : optional<ores::logging::logging_options>
                    +nats : ores::nats::config::nats_options
                    +database : ores::database::database_options
                }
                class parser {
                }
            }
        }
    }
}

' --- manual: everything below this line is hand-authored; the script preserves it ---

' The automated pass reads data members, so a class whose content is methods arrives
' as an empty box, and the messaging namespace -- two generated registrars and
' nothing else -- does not arrive at all.
'
' Fully-qualified class names rather than a reopened namespace block, matching the
' other diagrams in this tree. Redeclaring a class merges its members into the box
' the automated pass already drew.

class ores::workflow::service::app::application <<hand-written>> {
    +application()
    +{static} make_context(db_opts : database_options) : database::context
    +run(io_ctx, cfg : options) : awaitable<void>
}

class ores::workflow::service::app::host <<hand-written>> {
    +{static} execute(args : vector<string>) : awaitable<int>
}

class ores::workflow::service::config::parser <<hand-written>> {
    +parse(arguments : vector<string>, info, error) : options
}

' The whole messaging namespace, which the automated pass does not draw because it
' holds two free functions and no class. Each generated registrar owns one entity's
' event mapping: it reads the entity's NOTIFY channel and publishes the canonical
' event on the subject the action names.
'
' It is drawn as a package of its own rather than nested inside ores.workflow.service,
' because a namespace the automated pass never opened cannot be reopened here: doing
' so creates a second package rather than merging into the first. The label carries
' the full path, so the nesting is unambiguous either way.

class ores::workflow::service::messaging::register_workflow_instance_event_mapping <<generated free function>> {
    +{static} register_workflow_instance_event_mapping(event_source, event_bus, nats) : subscription
}

class ores::workflow::service::messaging::register_workflow_step_event_mapping <<generated free function>> {
    +{static} register_workflow_step_event_mapping(event_source, event_bus, nats) : subscription
}

' What application::run composes, in the order it has to happen.

"ores::workflow::service::app::application" ..> "ores::workflow::service::messaging::register_workflow_instance_event_mapping" : composes
"ores::workflow::service::app::application" ..> "ores::workflow::service::messaging::register_workflow_step_event_mapping" : composes
"ores::workflow::service::app::host" ..> "ores::workflow::service::app::application" : runs
"ores::workflow::service::app::host" ..> "ores::workflow::service::config::parser" : parses first

note right of "ores::workflow::service::app::application"
run() is the component's composition root, and its order
matters more than its contents.

The durable workflow JetStream stream is ensured before any
subscription, because the engine's two subjects are addressed
through it. The event bus and the postgres event source are
built next, then the two generated event mappings are
registered, and only then does the source start: a
subscription made after start() can miss the very change it
exists to catch. The subscriptions are held in locals rather
than discarded, because the source delivers into them.

The messaging registrar is handed to ores.service's runner
last, and that call blocks for the process's lifetime -- so
the event source is stopped after it returns, on the way out.
end note

